00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:31.690
Thank you guys so much. It's an honor to be here and to make music in this space at Tiny Desk.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:38.370
And it's an honor to be joined by some good friends and fabulous people and fabulous artists.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:49.910
We've got Donald Lee on piano, Lady Jess on violin, and Lizzie Steiner on harp.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:57.830
We're going to do a variety of different things for you today.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.230
So I'm an opera singer by trade.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.910
I have been singing opera since I was about 13 or 14 years old.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.270
I'm originally from Norfolk, Virginia.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:09.830
And my parents are both musical.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.230
My mom actually is a gospel singer.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.650
My dad played a little bit of jazz trumpet and piano,

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:19.890
but I'm so grateful that they introduced me to music

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.790
at an early age, one of the greatest gifts

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.930
they could have ever have given me.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.610
And as I go throughout my career,

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:34.210
outside of the music business, I think that music

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.250
doesn't really exist so much in the boxes

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.230
that we try to put it in when you think about classical

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:46.350
jazz, country folk, you know, I believe that music is just all about storytelling

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:52.830
and or music as a way to just connect, you know, to human emotions and the human

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.830
experience. So when it comes to opera, I was always interested in opera because of

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:04.030
the stories and the epic music and the costumes and sort of the grandeur of it

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:09.150
all. You know, I understand some people that don't really vibe with opera,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.470
There's the disconnect because our voices are a little,

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.590
it can be a little bit jarring and robust,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:18.470
but we sing that way because we're unamplified.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.830
A lot of the times we're on these stages

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.270
and we have to be our own microphone.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.990
And a lot of times we're singing in foreign languages,

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.970
but if you ever go to the opera,

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.630
there's always super titles

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.310
where you can follow along with the stories.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.070
And if you take a deeper dive into opera,

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:40.070
There's so many fascinating stories and connection points

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:43.670
to the human experience, stories of love and betrayal

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:47.590
and murder and all these different plot twists.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.310
And it's a really fascinating art form.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:53.430
And so this next piece that we're gonna do

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:57.950
is from an opera called Eugene Onyagin.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.390
And it's an opera in Russian.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.990
And if you know anything about opera,

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.590
it's not typical to hear Russian all that much,

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.730
at least on the state side.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.210
You know, you hear Italian, French, German, English,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.010
but you know, Russian is a little bit off the beaten path,

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.530
but I love it as a connoisseur of all music.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.310
This piece is by Tchaikovsky,

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.970
just gorgeous melodies and harmonies,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.250
and I love to study the language

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.610
and as it pertains to the singing.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.550
And to give you a little context of this song,

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.050
Eugene Unyagin, the title character,

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:36.230
There's this woman Tatiana who has fallen for him to the point where she's

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:42.110
actually professed her love to Eugene and he proceeds in this song to break down

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:46.710
the reasons why they can't be together and basically rejects her but it's like

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:52.790
the most beautiful melodious you know rejection you could ever get so and

00:06:52.790 --> 00:07:15.650
that's what opera is.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.820
I will pay for her

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:10.820
I will give you my life

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:21.150
And I will give you my land

00:08:21.150 --> 00:09:06.090
I will give you my land

00:11:11.430 --> 00:12:23.590
So, right around the time of the pandemic, I started to write more music.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:28.830
It's something I've always been interested in, but it takes a lot just to be an opera

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:34.270
singer with all the things we have to learn and do to perfect the craft.

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:36.430
But I started to write music as a means to escape.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:42.870
And I think sometimes I still process the pandemic with the people that we lost and just

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:43.870
the unknown.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:48.710
You know, for us artists, you know, having a live audience is a part of it.

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:55.150
So having that gone, you know, I feel like coming out of it, I have just a new, just

00:12:55.150 --> 00:13:01.350
gratitude for being around a live audience and feeling that energy is something I

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.950
will never take for granted again.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:08.750
But anyway, as I started to write more, making that more visible, a friend of mine asked me

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:17.070
to write a song for her based on a poem called A Golden Day with words by the poet Paul Dunbar.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:20.390
And if you're not familiar with Paul Dunbar, you should check him out.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:22.430
He is an amazing poet.

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:24.790
He's from Dayton, Ohio.

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:25.790
He died really young.

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:27.710
He was maybe 32 or 33.

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:33.430
one of the first black poets to receive national recognition.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:34.750
So after I wrote that song for her,

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:36.830
I kind of went down this spiral of looking

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:38.350
through all of Dunbar's works.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:42.030
And then I discovered that he had an ex-wife, Alice Moore,

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.390
who also wrote poems.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.910
And they're just stunning, stunning poems.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:49.790
And I did that because I think it's important

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.150
to preserve our black voices and black poets,

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.830
especially in classical music.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:59.190
And I think Paul and Alice's writing is a great example of black art.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.730
You know, that's not just all pain and suffering, you know, there's love and

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.770
there's beauty, the complications of love.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:08.830
Um, and it's just a really, really great writing.

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:13.390
And I'm, I did an album, a collection of, of, uh, songs based off of their

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:17.570
poems called the Dunbar, more sessions that I released a few years ago.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:22.410
And so we're going to perform, uh, a piece, Good Night, uh,

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.710
co-written by myself and Lady Jess.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:18:43.560
It's one thing to watch the Tiny Desk but actually be in it.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:52.420
I want to shout out Donald Lee for the arrangements of the Eugenia and Yeganaria for Harp and Violin

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:57.620
and this next piece that we're going to do, which is based off an arrangement by Hall Johnson,

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:04.820
entitled, Right on King Jesus. I love, love, love singing spirituals, and I approach them with a

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:10.980
great amount of respect because as you may know, you know, spirituals were born out of something

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:17.940
monstrous when you think about slavery. And it's also profound that these songs have turned

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:26.460
into something uplifting and healing. And from a musical aspect, I love doing

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.700
spirituals because growing up as a church kid, you know, there's so many

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:37.260
gospel undertones in that so it's like the perfect intersection for me. And,

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:43.820
you know, while I did not suffer what my ancestors went through, I think part

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:50.420
of what it is to be an artist is, you know, we inherit these traditions and these stories

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:53.380
and these songs and these gifts.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:57.980
I think it's our responsibility to carry them forward and to be of service to others.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:00.420
So thank you all so much for today.

